WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.867 --> 00:00:03.003
It is heartening to see some important

00:00:03.003 --> 00:00:04.004
Republican figures

00:00:04.004 --> 00:00:06.039
come out against Donald Trump,

00:00:06.039 --> 00:00:06.740
but it's worth

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:08.942
noting that many of them embraced him

00:00:08.942 --> 00:00:10.977
when he proposed a Muslim ban.

00:00:10.977 --> 00:00:12.379
Strong armed Ukraine's

00:00:12.379 --> 00:00:14.414
president was impeached

00:00:14.414 --> 00:00:16.549
and then tried to overturn an election.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:19.786
His real sin in their eyes is that

00:00:19.786 --> 00:00:21.988
now he is losing popularity.

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:25.458
However, Trump's slump among Republicans

00:00:25.458 --> 00:00:26.793
could change.

00:00:26.793 --> 00:00:29.095
Imagine that during the 2024 campaign,

00:00:29.095 --> 00:00:30.363
the Republican Party runs

00:00:30.363 --> 00:00:32.565
a large and varied field.

00:00:32.565 --> 00:00:33.433
Ron DeSantis,

00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:34.334
Mike Pence, Mike

00:00:34.334 --> 00:00:36.603
Pompeo, Nikki Haley, Larry Hogan

00:00:36.603 --> 00:00:37.537
and Liz Cheney,

00:00:37.537 --> 00:00:39.672
among other possible candidates,

00:00:39.672 --> 00:00:42.042
Trump would start with a shrunken base,

00:00:42.042 --> 00:00:44.511
but would generate enormous publicity

00:00:44.811 --> 00:00:46.179
and would likely win

00:00:46.179 --> 00:00:48.048
the single largest vote share

00:00:48.048 --> 00:00:49.716
in the early primaries.

00:00:49.716 --> 00:00:50.850
He might not get best

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:52.986
50% of the vote in any state,

00:00:53.286 --> 00:00:55.755
but most Republican state primary systems

00:00:55.955 --> 00:00:57.390
favor the front runner.

00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:59.092
And in state after state,

00:00:59.092 --> 00:01:01.628
he would just do better than anyone else.

00:01:02.128 --> 00:01:05.131
As Ron Brownstein reminds us, that is

00:01:05.131 --> 00:01:06.633
how Trump became the presumptive

00:01:06.633 --> 00:01:08.368
nominee in 2016.

00:01:08.368 --> 00:01:11.237
While only garnering around 40% of

00:01:11.237 --> 00:01:11.971
total votes,

00:01:13.173 --> 00:01:14.374
voters did deliver

00:01:14.374 --> 00:01:16.176
a powerful rebuke to the Republicans

00:01:16.176 --> 00:01:17.410
in the midterm elections.

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:19.446
And clearly it was centered around

00:01:19.446 --> 00:01:22.382
two issues election denial and abortion.

00:01:23.249 --> 00:01:25.718
But those who shifted appear

00:01:25.752 --> 00:01:27.020
to have been independents

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:29.222
and a sliver of moderate Republicans.

00:01:29.823 --> 00:01:31.257
These are not the voters

00:01:31.257 --> 00:01:31.991
who will determine

00:01:31.991 --> 00:01:34.294
the results of Republican primaries.

00:01:35.562 --> 00:01:37.530
The results also don't tell us enough

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:39.799
about a possible matchup between Trump

00:01:39.799 --> 00:01:41.101
and DeSantis.

00:01:41.101 --> 00:01:42.302
DeSantis victory

00:01:42.302 --> 00:01:42.936
in the Florida

00:01:42.936 --> 00:01:45.371
gubernatorial race was impressive,

00:01:45.371 --> 00:01:46.806
but in the early stages

00:01:46.806 --> 00:01:48.541
of a presidential campaign,

00:01:48.541 --> 00:01:50.276
DeSantis would not be facing

00:01:50.276 --> 00:01:52.112
Trump mano a mano,

00:01:52.112 --> 00:01:54.614
but rather as one choice among many.

00:01:55.148 --> 00:01:55.849
A New York Times

00:01:55.849 --> 00:01:57.884
Siena poll from October found

00:01:57.884 --> 00:02:00.620
that almost half of likely primary voters

00:02:00.787 --> 00:02:02.388
still prefer Trump,

00:02:02.388 --> 00:02:03.423
with about a quarter

00:02:03.423 --> 00:02:04.724
favoring DeSantis

00:02:04.724 --> 00:02:07.260
and only 6% favoring Pence.

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:08.795
DeSantis is popularity

00:02:08.795 --> 00:02:10.096
will probably have increased

00:02:10.096 --> 00:02:11.331
in recent weeks,

00:02:11.331 --> 00:02:12.832
but in a possible 20,

00:02:12.832 --> 00:02:15.101
24 presidential campaign,

00:02:15.101 --> 00:02:17.971
he will be fighting for not Trump voters

00:02:18.171 --> 00:02:19.606
and the not Trump

00:02:19.606 --> 00:02:21.541
lane of the Republican Party

00:02:21.541 --> 00:02:24.110
is going to get very crowded

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:24.644
with Trump

00:02:24.644 --> 00:02:26.646
to have a revival of his fortunes.

00:02:26.913 --> 00:02:29.582
Many would jump back on his bandwagon.

00:02:29.883 --> 00:02:31.684
Ted Cruz has already reserved

00:02:31.684 --> 00:02:32.785
a spot there,

00:02:32.785 --> 00:02:33.820
promising that if Trump

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:35.221
were to get the nomination,

00:02:35.221 --> 00:02:37.423
he would enthusiastically support him

00:02:38.358 --> 00:02:40.660
Republican officials seem to be hoping

00:02:40.927 --> 00:02:42.028
that their voters

00:02:42.028 --> 00:02:43.530
will do their dirty work

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:45.832
for them and deliver them from Trump.

00:02:46.366 --> 00:02:49.169
Reversing the usual roles of leaders

00:02:49.169 --> 00:02:50.503
and followers.

00:02:50.503 --> 00:02:51.771
But it won't work.

00:02:51.771 --> 00:02:53.806
The party must put an end

00:02:53.806 --> 00:02:55.642
to its moral cowardice.

00:02:55.642 --> 00:02:57.977
And finally, and frontally confront

00:02:57.977 --> 00:02:59.512
the cancer within.

00:02:59.512 --> 00:03:00.813
Republican leaders

00:03:00.813 --> 00:03:02.882
need to explain to their voters

00:03:02.882 --> 00:03:04.651
that Trump is a demagog

00:03:04.651 --> 00:03:06.319
who tried to undermine

00:03:06.319 --> 00:03:07.954
American democracy,

00:03:07.954 --> 00:03:08.855
which should make him

00:03:08.855 --> 00:03:10.023
an unacceptable

00:03:10.023 --> 00:03:12.058
nominee for the Republican Party.

00:03:12.792 --> 00:03:13.960
In a fascinating essay

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:14.427
in Foreign

00:03:14.427 --> 00:03:15.061
Affairs,

00:03:15.061 --> 00:03:16.930
Barnard College scholar Sheri

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:18.331
Berman points out

00:03:18.331 --> 00:03:19.098
that America

00:03:19.098 --> 00:03:20.800
is something of an outlier

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:22.802
among well-established democracies.

00:03:23.236 --> 00:03:24.804
She notes that in many Western

00:03:24.804 --> 00:03:25.972
European countries,

00:03:25.972 --> 00:03:26.639
right wing

00:03:26.639 --> 00:03:28.508
populist parties have been forced

00:03:28.508 --> 00:03:29.642
to retreat

00:03:29.642 --> 00:03:31.444
from their most extreme positions

00:03:31.444 --> 00:03:34.013
and accept mainstream stances on issues

00:03:34.214 --> 00:03:35.682
such as the European Union,

00:03:35.682 --> 00:03:38.151
the euro and the war in Ukraine.

00:03:38.151 --> 00:03:40.587
But in the US, the Republican Party,

00:03:40.587 --> 00:03:41.487
having opted

00:03:41.487 --> 00:03:42.522
for extremism

00:03:42.522 --> 00:03:44.591
in the wake of the Trump revolution,

00:03:44.591 --> 00:03:45.992
has been far more willing

00:03:45.992 --> 00:03:48.027
to go along with his dictates.

00:03:48.027 --> 00:03:50.263
With a slate of almost 300

00:03:50.263 --> 00:03:51.297
election deniers

00:03:51.297 --> 00:03:53.566
as candidates in the midterms,

00:03:53.566 --> 00:03:55.134
the reason Berman points out

00:03:55.134 --> 00:03:55.902
both in the essay

00:03:55.902 --> 00:03:57.737
and an economist session with me,

00:03:57.737 --> 00:03:59.272
is that the institutions

00:03:59.272 --> 00:04:01.241
and norms of liberal democracy

00:04:01.241 --> 00:04:03.209
are strong in Western Europe.

00:04:03.209 --> 00:04:05.311
The political parties act responsibly.

00:04:05.578 --> 00:04:06.212
European

00:04:06.212 --> 00:04:08.081
and national institutions maintain

00:04:08.081 --> 00:04:09.182
their independence,

00:04:09.182 --> 00:04:11.484
and leaders call out bad behavior.

00:04:11.884 --> 00:04:13.553
So from Sweden to Italy,

00:04:13.553 --> 00:04:14.687
when radical right

00:04:14.687 --> 00:04:16.422
wing parties come to power,

00:04:16.422 --> 00:04:18.791
they are rarely able to change policy

00:04:18.791 --> 00:04:20.193
along the dramatic lines

00:04:20.193 --> 00:04:21.828
that they had once called for.

00:04:21.828 --> 00:04:23.296
She notes that in Sweden and Italy,

00:04:23.296 --> 00:04:23.896
the far right

00:04:23.896 --> 00:04:25.465
parties have had to moderate

00:04:25.465 --> 00:04:27.033
their rhetoric and policies

00:04:27.033 --> 00:04:29.435
significantly to attract support

00:04:29.736 --> 00:04:31.871
and be seen as serious enough to govern.

00:04:32.972 --> 00:04:33.673
The United States,

00:04:33.673 --> 00:04:36.142
unfortunately, has a weaker, more open

00:04:36.142 --> 00:04:38.111
political system to begin with,

00:04:38.111 --> 00:04:39.145
defined nowadays

00:04:39.145 --> 00:04:40.880
by primaries, money,

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:42.882
social media and celebrity,

00:04:43.249 --> 00:04:44.417
all of which enable

00:04:44.417 --> 00:04:46.085
an entrepreneurial politician

00:04:46.085 --> 00:04:47.220
like Trump

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:49.555
to take over a major political party

00:04:49.756 --> 00:04:51.224
and turn it into something

00:04:51.224 --> 00:04:53.626
resembling a personality cult.

00:04:53.626 --> 00:04:54.394
At the last

00:04:54.394 --> 00:04:56.529
Republican National Convention,

00:04:56.529 --> 00:04:58.998
not one of the former presidents

00:04:58.998 --> 00:04:59.999
or presidential

00:04:59.999 --> 00:05:02.335
nominees of the party spoke,

00:05:02.335 --> 00:05:04.771
but six members of Trump's family

00:05:05.071 --> 00:05:06.873
were given prominence rights,

00:05:06.873 --> 00:05:08.241
something unthinkable

00:05:08.241 --> 00:05:10.243
in a European country.

00:05:10.243 --> 00:05:10.910
Bowman writes

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:11.911
that Freedom House

00:05:11.911 --> 00:05:12.779
and other groups

00:05:12.779 --> 00:05:14.914
that track Democratic development,

00:05:14.914 --> 00:05:16.983
such as V Dem have noted

00:05:16.983 --> 00:05:18.318
a marked decline

00:05:18.318 --> 00:05:20.386
in the strength of American democracy,

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:21.421
but have found

00:05:21.421 --> 00:05:23.556
no similar decline in Western Europe.

00:05:25.124 --> 00:05:25.792
In countries

00:05:25.792 --> 00:05:27.493
where democratic institutions

00:05:27.493 --> 00:05:28.561
are weaker,

00:05:28.561 --> 00:05:29.629
such as Hungary,

00:05:29.629 --> 00:05:31.898
Turkey and alas, nowadays

00:05:31.898 --> 00:05:35.702
the United States demagogs change parties

00:05:36.035 --> 00:05:37.570
rather than the other way around

00:05:38.838 --> 00:05:40.506
to fend off this threat.

00:05:40.506 --> 00:05:41.708
Republican leaders

00:05:41.708 --> 00:05:45.244
must act to purge their party and country

00:05:45.478 --> 00:05:48.514
of extremism even after the midterms.

00:05:48.915 --> 00:05:50.550
Trump and Trumpism

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:52.618
will not magically vanish.

